# TypeScript And Aliases

- All application code must be written in TypeScript (`.ts` / `.tsx`).
- Avoid creating new JavaScript source files (`.js` / `.jsx`) in `src`.
- Use path aliases from `tsconfig.json` for cross-layer imports. Each layer uses `@layer/*` (e.g. `@application/*`, `@domain/*`, `@infrastructure/*`, `@http/*`, `@ai/*`, `@config/*`).
- Include the `.js` extension in alias imports (NodeNext / ESM).
- Import from `@helpers` for the helpers barrel; use `@helpers/*` only when importing a specific helper file not re-exported by the barrel.
- Avoid deep relative paths like `../../application/...` when a layer alias exists.
- Within the same layer folder, prefer relative imports to avoid unnecessary coupling. This includes:
  - `application/*` importing sibling files under `application/` (e.g. `./patient-chat-interaction-groups.js`)
  - `ai/prompts/*` importing sibling prompts (e.g. `./patient-chat-system.js`)
  - `infrastructure/supabase/*` importing sibling repositories (e.g. `./clinical-content.repository.js`)
  - `domain/*` importing sibling types (e.g. `./types.js`)

## Example

```ts
// ✅ cross-layer — use alias with .js extension
import { endPsychologistSession } from '@application/end-psychologist-session.js';
import type { AppLanguage } from '@domain/types.js';
import { createAdminSupabaseClient } from '@infrastructure/supabase/client.js';
import { keysToCamel } from '@helpers';

// ✅ same layer — relative sibling
import { splitChatIntoInteractions } from './patient-chat-interaction-groups.js';
import { buildPatientChatSystemPrompt } from './patient-chat-system.js';

// ❌ cross-layer via relative path
import { PATIENT_CHAT_WEEKLY_LOOKBACK_MS } from '../../application/patient-chat-interaction-groups.js';
import { NotFoundError } from '../application/authorization.js';
```
